A||N||7||pollock||ECM replacement- ripoff?||||5||
Z||000000||pollock||06-11-2001||04:22 PM||||My buddy took his car in for diagnostics and repairs.  He was told it needed a new ECM and was quoted a price.  Later he got a call and was told that when they took the old ECM out, the eprom "lost its memory," and would also have to be replaced. <P>Is he getting screwed?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||06-11-2001||04:29 PM||||Yes.  They're B.S.'ing you, or they're idiots.  The EPROM is READ ONLY and can't be erased accidentally.  It can be screwed up by an idiot, though.  Also, the memory in the ECM clears every time you unplug the battery.  This is normal. It relearns automatically when you restart the car.  Tell them to explain how an Eraseable Programmible Read Only Memory chip lost it's memory.  You need special hardware to erase them.  It doesn't happen by accident.  Also, when you get your "new" ECM, make damn sure they give you the old one too, and then get it tested.  If you have access to the car, I'd even consider making a small mark somewhere on the ECM just to see if they're gonna change it or just give you a wall job.  Sounds like they're trying to rip you off.||||reg||13
Z||000002||Formula88||06-11-2001||04:30 PM||||Oh, and what kind of price are they quoting?  Swapping the ECM is VERY EASY!!!  And I'm sure someone on here can help you find the right one for under $100.  Maybe under $60.||||reg||4
Z||000003||Alex4mula||06-11-2001||04:34 PM||||Big time!! I bet they don't have a clue what the problem is and started to change components. Of course they start with the most expensive. PROMs need to be very mishandled to damage them with ESD or something to make them inoperable. Maybe they did the damage. To me that's pure BS. Take it to other place.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ROB MAC||06-12-2001||02:00 AM||||Some scrap yards sell used ECM's for $50-70 bucks guaranteed to work. That beats the $250 I paid for a "REBUILT" one that crapped out in a week and had to be replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroluke||06-15-2001||09:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ROB MAC:<BR><B>Some scrap yards sell used ECM's for $50-70 bucks guaranteed to work. That beats the $250 I paid for a "REBUILT" one that crapped out in a week and had to be replaced.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, sometimes rebuilt is better than junkyard! If the ECM in question uses surface mount parts (like for instance the '748 does), and is not a rebuilt unit, chances are that it may fail too. This is because these were built with a defective coating, which can literally tear off parts off the ECM as it ages. Needless to say, this can result in all kinds of strange behavior, and does not necessarily need to set a code!!! GM never officially admitted this (recall nightmare!), but look at remanufacturer websites for details about the problem. Some codes show a diagnostic hint saying "tap on ECM, if code sets, replace ECM". This is GM's way of determining a problem with their coating, which is obviously very non-scientific and unreliable.<P>If you get a factory remanufactured unit from a junkyard, you should be set: but if the new coat is better than the old one remainds to be seen<P><P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/th_88gt.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000006||theogre||06-15-2001||07:38 PM||||You can get the 1227748 ECM from a number of GM cars. If it has the DIS motor it almost always has this ECM. Just make sure the thing has it's label if you can. <P>The PROM is a bit more dificult. The PROM has to be from a car that has the same transmission and VSS setup as Fiero. One I know for sure you can't use is Grand Am. VSS is programmed differently and won't work with Fiero. You could try an Olds Cutlass Caliase. I haven't tested one of those yet.<P>If the VSS is programmed wrong it won't damage anything... However Cruise control will not work and you'll set codes in the ECM.<P>There is NO way a PROM can loose it's programming unless it is misshandled by the shop.  <P>The ECM "defect" is discussed on the A1 Cardone web site. <A HREF="http://www.cardone.com/english/club/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cardone.com/english/club/</A> <P>I can verify that there is a question of construction quality on the ECM's. Many of them, not just 1227748. There is one forum member that I know for sure had this problem. In his case he kept getting error 42 until the ECM was finally replaced.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||06-15-2001||07:43 PM||||By the time I read all this I thought we were talking about a 87-88 Fiero 4 cyl... I'm tired....<P>Pollock, I depends on just what car and ECM or PCM is involved. It's posible the newer PCM units could loose the progamming but that also indicates incompetance. The new PCM's use FLASH RAM that is reprogramable. If someone botch a flash job then the PCM can go brain dead.<P>In cases like Fiero ECM's, the programming is PROM and then you are back to my above post.||||reg||1
